    "originalTitle": "Nano-Eutectic Systems for Solubility Enhancement of BCS Class II Drugs: Formulation and Evaluation of a Nano-Emulsified Deep Eutectic Solvent (NanoDES) for Spironolactone",
    "abstract": "Poor aqueous solubility remains one of the most significant challenges in pharmaceutical formulation, with nearly 40% of approved drugs and up to 90% of pipeline compounds classified as poorly water-soluble. Spironolactone, a BCS Class II aldosterone antagonist used for heart failure, hypertension, and hyperaldosteronism, has an aqueous solubility of approximately 28 mg/L, leading to erratic oral bioavailability and therapeutic inconsistency. This study proposes a novel green chemistry approach the development of a Nano-Emulsified Deep Eutectic Solvent (NanoDES) using a hydrophobic eutectic system formed from two pharmaceutically acceptable, GRAS-listed solid components: Menthol and Camphor (1:1 molar ratio). These two solids spontaneously liquefy upon mixing, forming a hydrophobic liquid that acts as a powerful solvent for insoluble drugs. Spironolactone will be dissolved in the resulting eutectic liquid, and this drug-loaded liquid will subsequently be nano-emulsified using food grade surfactants (Tween 80 and PEG 400) to yield stable nanodroplets below 200 nm. The formulation will be characterized for droplet size, zeta potential, and drug loading, and evaluated through comparative dissolution studies against pure drug powder. The NanoDES system offers a solvent-free, non-toxic, and scalable strategy to dramatically enhance drug solubility and oral bioavailability through principles of green pharmaceutical chemistry.",
