    "abstract": "This research proposal explores the transition of menstrual blood from a ritual waste product to a therapeutic tool in Pakistan, focusing on the potential of Menstrual Blood-derived Mesenchymal Stem Cells (MenSCs) for treating chronic diseases like diabetes and heart disease. The study aims to evaluate the knowledge, attitudes, and willingness of Pakistani women (ages 18–45) to donate menstrual blood, specifically addressing the barrier of using menstrual cups, which are required for sterile collection and high cell viability. By investigating the intersection of Islamic bioethics, cultural modesty, and medical myths such as the \"virginity taboo\" and the concept of ritual impurity, this cross-sectional survey seeks to identify how education on life-saving medical potential can overcome existing sociological barriers. Ultimately, the research intends to provide the groundwork for a donor framework in Pakistan, shifting the narrative of menstruation toward a \"biomedical resource\" and a sustainable \"gift of life\".",
