    "originalTitle": "Virtual Screening for Nipah Virus RNA Polymerase (L Protein) Inhibitors: A Computational Drug Repurposing Approach",
    "abstract": "Nipah virus (NiV) is a highly lethal zoonotic pathogen causing severe encephalitis with mortality rates exceeding 70%, yet no approved antiviral treatments exist. The viral L protein, an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase essential for viral replication, represents an attractive therapeutic target due to its high conservation and critical function. This study proposes a comprehensive in-silico virtual screening approach to identify potential L protein inhibitors from FDA-approved drug libraries and natural product databases. Utilizing the recently resolved cryo-EM structure of NiV L protein (PDB: 9FUX), we will perform high-throughput molecular docking of approximately 10,000 compounds, followed by rigorous ADMET profiling with emphasis on blood-brain barrier permeability. Top-scoring candidates will undergo molecular dynamics simulation validation to confirm binding stability. The study aims to identify 5-10 lead compounds suitable for rapid drug repurposing, potentially accelerating therapeutic development for future Nipah virus outbreaks. This computational framework will establish a foundation for structure-based drug discovery against emerging paramyxoviruses while providing immediately actionable results for pandemic preparedness.",
