    "originalTitle": "In-Silico Design, Molecular Docking, and ADMET Profiling of Novel Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4 (DPP-4) Inhibitor Analogs for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus",
    "abstract": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM) is a rapidly escalating public health crisis. Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors are a vital class of oral antidiabetic drugs utilized in its management. Despite the availability of these drugs, patients can experience variable efficacy, and there is a continuous demand for affordable novel structural analogs with superior binding affinity and improved pharmacokinetic profiles. This project aims to utilize computational chemistry to identify potential new drug candidates through in-silico design, molecular docking, and ADMET profiling. The methodology involves designing 10 to 20 novel structural analogs of existing DPP-4 inhibitors. After retrieving and preparing the human DPP-4 enzyme from the Protein Data Bank , high-throughput virtual screening will be performed using PyRx and AutoDock Vina to evaluate theoretical binding affinities. The best docking poses will be visualized to analyze hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic interactions, and pi-pi stacking. Furthermore, top hit compounds will undergo ADMET profiling via online servers to ensure drug-likeness and pharmacokinetic viability. The study expects to identify 1 to 3 novel structural analogs demonstrating stronger theoretical binding affinities than currently marketed drugs. These findings will provide predictive data to serve as a foundation for future wet-lab synthesis and in-vitro pharmacological testing.",
