    "originalTitle": "Assessment of Self-Initiated Supplement Use and Social Media Influence Among Adults in the General Population",
    "abstract": "The growing use of dietary supplements without professional guidance has become a public health concern, particularly due to the influence of social media platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, and YouTube. Many adults consume vitamins, minerals, herbal products, and protein supplements based on online information and influencer recommendations rather than medical advice, which may lead to inappropriate use, adverse effects, and drug–supplement interactions.This study aims to assess the prevalence of self-initiated supplement use and examine the influence of social media among adults in Karachi, Pakistan. A cross-sectional descriptive study will be conducted on approximately 200–300 participants aged 18 years and above using a structured, self-administered questionnaire. The survey will collect data on demographics, supplement consumption patterns, social media exposure, and awareness of potential risks.Data will be analyzed using SPSS, with descriptive statistics and the Chi-square test applied to determine associations between variables. Ethical considerations, including informed consent and confidentiality, will be maintained.The findings are expected to provide insight into supplement use behaviors and the role of social media in shaping health decisions, supporting the development of awareness strategies and promoting safe and rational use of supplements.",
