    "abstract": "Sleep disturbances are increasingly prevalent among young adults due to academic pressure, psychological stress, irregular sleep routines, and lifestyle changes. This has contributed to a growing reliance on sleeping pills, including both prescription and over-the-counter medications. While these agents may provide short-term relief, inappropriate or prolonged use is associated with risks such as dependence, tolerance, and impaired cognitive function. This study aims to determine the prevalence and analyze the usage patterns of sleeping pill use among young adults, while presenting an evidence-oriented perspective on alternative, non-pharmacological therapies for sleep management. A descriptive cross-sectional study will be conducted with a target sample size of 300 participants. The findings are expected to support safer and more rational approaches to sleep management.",
