    "abstract": "The increasing reliance on medications and supplements as immediate solutions for everyday discomforts has given rise to a \"pharmacological quick-fix culture,\" particularly among young adults. This study aims to evaluate the prevalence, behavioural drivers, and safety implications of such practices among university students in Karachi, with a comparative focus on healthcare and non-healthcare disciplines. A cross-sectional study design will be employed, targeting undergraduate students from multiple universities in Karachi. Data will be collected through a structured, self-administered questionnaire assessing frequency of self-medication, types of pharmacological agents used (e.g., analgesics, sleep aids, laxatives, energy supplements), reasons for use, awareness of risks, and sources of information. Comparative analysis will be conducted to determine differences in knowledge, attitudes, and practices between healthcare and non-healthcare students.It is anticipated that the study will reveal a high prevalence of quick-fix pharmacotherapy, driven by academic stress, convenience, peer influence, and digital media exposure. While healthcare students may demonstrate higher awareness of medication risks, they may also exhibit increased self-medication due to greater confidence in their knowledge. The findings are expected to highlight gaps in safe medication practices and underscore the need for targeted educational interventions, stricter regulation of OTC access, and improved counselling at the community level. This study will contribute to understanding emerging medication-use behaviours among young adults in Pakistan and provide evidence to support policy and awareness strategies aimed at promoting rational pharmacotherapy.",
