    "originalTitle": "Impact of Ultra-Processed Snack Consumption on Early Visceral Fat Accumulation and Cognitive Functions in Young Adults",
    "abstract": "The consumption of ultra-processed snacks has risen sharply among young adults, driven by convenience and aggressive marketing. These products — including energy bars, chips, sugary drinks, and packaged baked goods — are characterized by high energy density, refined sugars, unhealthy fats, preservatives, and artificial additives, while being nutritionally poor. Emerging evidence links habitual intake of ultra-processed foods to adverse metabolic and neurological outcomes, yet data specific to young adults remain limited. This study investigates the relationship between ultra-processed snack consumption and early markers of metabolic and cognitive dysfunction in young adults aged 18–45 years. We assess dietary intake patterns and correlate them with visceral fat accumulation measured via bioelectrical impedance and waist-to-height ratio, alongside biochemical markers of oxidative stress and systemic inflammation. The research aims to determine whether frequent consumption of ultra-processed snacks is associated with increased visceral adiposity, elevated oxidative stress and inflammatory markers, and early signs of cognitive decline. Findings will clarify the mechanistic links between diet, metabolic health, and brain function in early adulthood, and inform preventive strategies against long-term risks of metabolic syndrome and neurodegenerative disorders.",
