    "originalTitle": "Assessment of Physicians Knowledge, Attitudes, and Clinical Practices Regarding Vitamin D Deficiency in Migraine Management: A Multi-centre Survey Based Study in Pakistan.",
    "abstract": "Migraine is a highly disabling neurovascular disorder and a leading cause of years lived with disability worldwide. Emerging evidence highlights the role of vitamin D’s immunomodulatory and anti-inflammatory effects in neurological health, with studies suggesting that low serum vitamin D levels correlate with increased migraine frequency and severity. Despite the high prevalence of vitamin D deficiency in Pakistan due to dietary and cultural factors, there is limited data regarding how healthcare professionals perceive and apply this association in clinical settings.This study aims to assess the knowledge, attitudes, and clinical practices of physicians and pharmacists in Pakistan concerning the role of vitamin D in migraine management. Key objectives include evaluating the frequency of vitamin D screening and supplementation, comparing practice patterns between the two professional groups, and identifying perceived barriers to integrated care .A multicenter, cross-sectional, questionnaire-based survey will be conducted in public and private tertiary care hospitals across major Pakistani cities. The study population will consist of licensed physicians and hospital pharmacists directly involved in migraine patient care . Data will be collected through a structured, self-administered questionnaire covering demographics, knowledge of the vitamin D–migraine link, clinical screening and referral practices, and attitudes toward interdisciplinary collaboration. The research is expected to identify significant knowledge gaps and variations in clinical practice regarding vitamin D assessment in migraine patients . By uncovering barriers such as a lack of formal guidelines or limited awareness, the findings will serve to inform targeted educational interventions and support the development of evidence-based, collaborative clinical care pathways to improve migraine management in Pakistan.",
