    "originalTitle": "Comparative Analysis of Perceived Effectiveness and Actual Clinical Outcomes of Herbal Medicines with Emphasis on the Placebo Effect",
    "abstract": "Abstract Aim\r\nThe aim of this study is to evaluate the difference between patients’ perceived effectiveness of herbal medicines and their actual clinical outcomes, with a focus on understanding the role of belief (placebo effect) in treatment response.\r\nMethodology\r\nA cross-sectional survey will be conducted using a structured Google Form questionnaire. Data will be collected from approximately 25-50 participants who have previously used herbal medicines. The questionnaire will include sections on demographic information, types of herbal remedies used, perceived improvement, actual symptom outcomes, and belief-related perceptions. The collected data will be analyzed using descriptive statistics, and results will be presented in the form of tables, pie charts, and bar graphs to compare perceived effectiveness with actual clinical outcomes.\r\nPredicted Results\r\nIt is expected that a higher proportion of participants will report perceived improvement compared to those who experience complete clinical recovery. Some participants may still require allopathic treatment despite initial use of herbal remedies. The findings may also indicate that belief and expectation play a significant role in how patients perceive the effectiveness of herbal medicines.\r\nPredicted Conclusion\r\nThe study is expected to conclude that perceived benefits of herbal medicines often exceed actual clinical outcomes, suggesting a possible influence of the placebo effect. It may also highlight the importance of clinical pharmacists in educating patients about evidence-based use of herbal medicines to ensure rational therapy and prevent delays in proper medical treatment.",
