    "originalTitle": "Clinical Evaluation of Treatment Patterns, Safety, and Patient Counselling in the Management of Vitiligo: A Patient-Centred Study",
    "abstract": "Abstract \r\nThis patient-centered pharmacy study evaluates current vitiligo management by analyzing treatment patterns, safety profiles, and the quality of patient counseling. Utilizing existing clinical data and prescription reviews, the research identifies that while conventional therapies—such as corticosteroids and calcineurin inhibitors—are frequently employed, they are often associated with skin thinning, irritation, and diminished long-term efficacy. Results indicate that poor patient adherence is a significant barrier, driven by delayed therapeutic results and inadequate guidance from healthcare providers. Conversely, a literary comparison suggests that herbal treatments may offer superior safety and tolerance as complementary options. The study concludes that an integrative approach is necessary, highlighting the vital role of pharmacists in implementing structured counseling plans—covering application techniques and lifestyle adjustments—to improve medication adherence and overall clinical outcomes.",
