    "abstract": "Abstract\r\nAccess to affordable healthcare remains a significant challenge, particularly in low- and middle-income countries, where medication costs can limit treatment adherence. Generic medicines, being therapeutically equivalent to branded drugs, offer a cost-effective alternative; however, their utilization is often hindered by limited patient awareness, lack of trust, and low acceptance. This study aims to assess the awareness and use of generic medicines among patients visiting community pharmacies, with a focus on the role of trust, affordability, and acceptance in promoting cost-effective therapy.\r\nA cross-sectional descriptive study will be conducted among adult patients in community pharmacy settings using a structured questionnaire. The study will evaluate patient knowledge, perceptions of quality and efficacy, affordability considerations, and the influence of pharmacist recommendations on the acceptance and use of generic medicines. Additionally, the relationship between these factors and medication adherence will be explored.\r\nThe expected outcomes include identifying gaps in awareness, understanding the impact of trust and cost on patient decision-making, and highlighting the critical role of pharmacists in promoting generic substitution. The findings will provide valuable insights for healthcare policy and practice by supporting strategies to enhance the acceptance of generic medicines, reduce financial burden, and improve access to cost-effective therapy.",
