    "supervisor": "Dr. Sadia Shafiq RPh",
    "originalTitle": "Herbal Anti-Migraine magic wand: Formulation and Evaluation of Herbal Anti-Migraine Topical Gel Presented as a Relief wand",
    "abstract": "Migraine is a prevalent neurological disorder that significantly affects daily functioning and is often associated with symptoms such as nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia, which may limit the use of oral medications. This project focuses on the formulation and evaluation of a herbal anti-migraine topical gel developed under the name Calmora, designed in a convenient glide-based applicator for rapid and localized relief. The formulation incorporates Boswellia serrata extract as the primary active ingredient due to its anti-inflammatory properties, along with menthol and peppermint oil to provide a cooling effect and enhance patient comfort. A suitable gelling agent is employed to achieve optimal consistency and ease of application. The formulation is intended for topical application to the temples or forehead, enabling targeted action while avoiding gastrointestinal limitations associated with oral therapy. The prepared gel will be evaluated for physicochemical parameters including pH, viscosity, spreadability, homogeneity, and stability, along with user acceptability. Additionally, a pre-marketing survey will be conducted to assess consumer needs and acceptance of a topical herbal alternative. The study aims to develop a practical, patient-friendly, and innovative approach for migraine relief with potential for future development and commercialization.",
