    "originalTitle": "Rapid-Disintegrating Electrolyte Films for Patient-Centered Oral Rehydration",
    "abstract": "Background: Oral thin films (OTFs) are rapidly dissolving dosage forms that disintegrate on the tongue without the need for water, offering significant advantages for populations with swallowing difficulties, particularly pediatric and geriatric patients. Previous studies have demonstrated that OTFs are acceptable, easy to administer, and can be manufactured using pharmaceutically robust polymers and scalable processes.\r\n\r\nObjective: To design, manufacture, and evaluate “oral electrolyte dissolving strips” delivering a balanced electrolyte profile for rapid rehydration, with focus on pediatric suitability, stability, and palatability.\r\n\r\nMethods: Electrolyte-loaded OTFs will be prepared using the solvent casting method, employing hydroxypropyl methylcellulose (HPMC) and/or pullulan as film-forming polymers, along with glycerol or polyethylene glycol 400 (PEG 400) as plasticizers. Sweeteners (e.g., sucralose, xylitol) and child-friendly flavoring agents will be screened and optimized to enhance palatability while preserving rapid disintegration. The films (2 × 3 cm) will be formulated to contain essential electrolytes (Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, citrate) in balanced proportions. Quality control tests will include disintegration time, mechanical strength, thickness uniformity, and content uniformity across ≥10 units in accordance with pharmacopeial standards.\r\n\r\nResults: It is hypothesized that the developed films will exhibit rapid disintegration",
