    "originalTitle": "Design and evaluation of in situ nasal gel of rizatriptan for migraines.",
    "abstract": "Migraine is a common neurological disorder characterized by recurrent attacks of severe headache, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and sensitivity to light and sound. Conventional oral formulations of antimigraine drugs may show delayed onset of action due to first-pass metabolism and poor patient compliance during migraine attacks. Intranasal drug delivery offers a promising alternative as it can bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism and provide rapid drug absorption and increased bioavailability. The present project aims to design and evaluate an in-situ nasal gel formulation of Rizatriptan for the effective management of migraine. The formulation is intended to remain in liquid form before administration and undergo gelation within the nasal cavity, thereby increasing residence time and improving drug absorption. Suitable polymers such as carbopol/HPMC will be used to achieve in-situ gel formation and enhance mucoadhesion. The formulated gel will be evaluated for parameters including pH, viscosity, gelation behavior, drug content, and in-vitro drug release. The proposed system is expected to improve nasal retention, provide controlled drug release, and enhance therapeutic effectiveness. Thus, the developed in-situ nasal gel may offer a promising approach for rapid and efficient migraine treatment.",
