    "originalTitle": "Formulation and In-Vitro Characterization of an Herbal In-Situ Gelling Floating System: A Novel Approach for Gastro-Retentive Management of Acid Reflux.",
    "abstract": "Background: Conventional liquid antacids have short gastric residence times. Gastro-retentive \"raft\" systems provide a physical barrier against acid reflux, but synthetic versions often rely on metallic salts that may cause long-term side effects. \r\nObjective: To design an \"in-situ\" gelling liquid containing Zingiber officinale (Ginger) that transforms into a floating raft upon contact with gastric acid. \r\nMethodology: An aqueous solution was prepared using Sodium Alginate as a gelling agent and Calcium Carbonate as a gas-generating agent. A standardized extract of Zingiber officinale was incorporated for its prokinetic and \r\nanti-inflammatory properties. The \"200% impact\" was demonstrated by pouring the formulation into 0.1N HCl (pH 1.2), simulating the gastric environment. \r\nResults: Upon contact with the acidic medium, the liquid underwent immediate gelation (<5 seconds). The liberation of CO2 gas allowed the gel to remain buoyant for over 12 hours (floating lag time <10 seconds). \r\nConclusion: This floating herbal system successfully combines the therapeutic benefits of pharmacognosy with advanced pharmaceutics, offering a prolonged, localized, and natural solution for gastrointestinal distress.",
